## Certification requirements

Clinical Coverage Policy 12B says an HIV Case Management provider must:

- Have a **documented successful record of three years of providing or managing HIV Case Management programs**;
- Ensure services are provided by **qualified case managers**;
- Ensure HIV case management is supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **Enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- Meet **applicable state and federal Medicaid provider-participation laws**;
- **Maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- Show compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- Show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- Allow **NC Medicaid or its DHHS-designated contractor** to review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- Notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and **no later than five business days of the actual change**; and
- **Within one year of Medicaid enrollment**, achieve **national accreditation** from one of the designated accrediting agencies: **ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC**.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.1 — “Certification Requirements” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Related staffing/training requirements tied to certification

Because Section 6.2.1 requires qualified staff and supervisors, the provider also has to meet the incorporated personnel standards.

### Supervisors
A supervisor must meet one of several listed education/experience pathways, including, for example:

- A **master’s degree** in a human services field plus **one year** of human services experience;
- A **BSW** plus **two years** of human services experience;
- A bachelor’s in a human services or related field with specified coursework plus **two years** of human services or public health experience;
- A bachelor’s degree plus **three years** of human services experience, or **two years plus one year of supervisory experience**; or
- Applicable NC licensure as an RN, NP, physician, PA, or certified substance abuse counselor, plus the required experience.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.4 — “HIV Case Management Supervisor” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### Training
The policy also requires training for case managers and supervisors covering at least:

- Basic HIV information and prevention techniques;
- The scope of work for case managers;
- The core components of HIV Case Management;
- Basic ethical issues relating to case management;
- Responsibilities and functions of the HIV case management system of care; and
- Documentation requirements.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.7.1 — “Training for Case Managers and Supervisors” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### Documentation and orders
The provider must maintain the documentation listed in the policy, including:

- Intake forms, with **proof of HIV status in the medical record within 15 calendar days of intake**;
- Assessments;
- Written attestation that records are properly and safely stored and available;
- Care plans with timelines;
- Progress notes;
- Medication sheet;
- Contact sheets for resource development and coordination;
- Discharge notification/summary, if applicable;
- Beneficiary consent forms; and
- A **physician or attending practitioner’s written order** before starting HIV case management, with a **Primary Care Physician’s written order required for services beyond two consecutive months**, indicating diagnosis and medical necessity.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 7.5 — “Documentation Requirements” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## When can a provider be decertified?

The policy lists the following grounds for **decertification**:

- **Failure to provide core service components**;
- **Fraudulent billing practices**;
- **Owner(s) being convicted of a felony charge**;
- Failure to **develop, submit, and implement a written plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **Falsification of records**;
- **Violation of a beneficiary’s confidentiality**;
- Employment of staff who **do not meet Section 6.1 criteria**;
- Failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- Failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units (CEUs)**;
- Failure to provide staff with supervision needed to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- Failure to submit required documentation on time;
- Failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing;
- Failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- Failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- Failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, State Plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- Failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- Failure to **achieve or maintain certification requirements** under Section 6.2.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.5 — “Decertification” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## How NC Medicaid monitors compliance

The policy also says NC Medicaid or its contractor may conduct **quality assurance site visits** that include:

- Medical record review and comparison with billing records;
- Review of **staff qualifications and training**;
- Chart review of case management services and documentation;
- Investigation of complaints; and
- Review of quality improvement activities.

That matters because these reviews are one of the ways certification compliance problems may be identified.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 7.8 — “Quality Assurance Site Visits” (effective Jan. 15, 2020): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Bottom line

A provider must be certified by meeting **experience, staffing, supervision, site enrollment, legal compliance, quality-improvement, record-review, notice, and accreditation requirements**. A provider can be decertified for failures in any of those areas, especially **fraud, confidentiality violations, unqualified staff, missed training/CEUs, inadequate documentation, failure to correct deficiencies, or failure to maintain certification standards**.

